{"id":"pearson-correlation-matrix","t":"Pearson correlation matrix","tags":["Probability and Statistics","Linear Algebra"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"corr_matrix","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"The Pearson correlation between features $a$ and $b$ is their covariance divided by the product of their standard deviations:\n\n$$r_{ab} = \\frac{\\sum_i (x_{ia} - \\bar x_a)(x_{ib} - \\bar x_b)}{\\sqrt{\\sum_i (x_{ia} - \\bar x_a)^2}\\,\\sqrt{\\sum_i (x_{ib} - \\bar x_b)^2}}$$\n\nWrite `corr_matrix(X)` that returns the full $d \\times d$ matrix $R$ for a data matrix whose **columns** are features.\n\n## Requirements\n- `X`: float array of shape `(n, d)` with $n \\ge 2$. Return a float array of shape `(d, d)`.\n- Diagonal entries are exactly `1.0`.\n- A **constant** column has no defined correlation. Use `0.0` for every off-diagonal entry in its row and column (its diagonal entry is still `1.0`). `np.corrcoef` gives `nan` here, so it won't match.\n- Use matrix products, not loops over pairs of columns.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef corr_matrix(X):\n    \"\"\"X: (n, d), columns are features. Return the (d, d) correlation matrix.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Correlation is the cosine similarity of the **centred** columns. The $1/(n-1)$ factors cancel, so you never need them.","Centre, scale each column to unit length, then one matrix product `Z.T @ Z` gives every pair at once.","A constant column centres to all zeros. Divide it by 1 instead of 0: its products with everything are then 0. Finally overwrite the diagonal with 1."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef corr_matrix(X):\n    X = np.asarray(X, dtype=float)\n    Xc = X - X.mean(axis=0)\n    norms = np.sqrt((Xc ** 2).sum(axis=0))\n    safe = np.where(norms > 0, norms, 1.0)          # constant column: its centred values are all 0\n    Z = Xc / safe                                   # unit-length centred columns (or a zero column)\n    R = Z.T @ Z                                     # cosine similarity of centred columns\n    np.fill_diagonal(R, 1.0)\n    return R\n","ex_md":"## Why it's a cosine\nAfter centring, $r_{ab} = \\frac{\\tilde x_a \\cdot \\tilde x_b}{\\lVert\\tilde x_a\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\tilde x_b\\rVert}$, the cosine of the angle between the two centred columns. That explains the range $[-1, 1]$ (Cauchy-Schwarz), why scaling or shifting a feature leaves $r$ unchanged, and why $|r| = 1$ means one column is an exact linear function of the other.\n\n## Complexity\nCentring is $O(nd)$ and the Gram matrix is $O(nd^2)$. Memory is $O(nd + d^2)$.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Forgetting to centre: you get cosine similarity, which depends on the mean.\n- Normalising rows instead of columns, which gives correlations between samples.\n- Leaving the diagonal as computed: it can come out as 0.9999999999999998, and a constant column gives 0 there.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Pearson only sees linear relationships. What would you use for a monotone but curved one? (Spearman: Pearson on the ranks.)\n- Two features have $r = 0.95$. Why might that hurt a linear regression, and what can you do? (Multicollinearity inflates coefficient variance. Drop one, combine them, or add ridge regularisation.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[1., 2., 3.],\n       [2., 4., 1.],\n       [3., 6., 2.]])","out":"array([[ 1. ,  1. , -0.5],\n       [ 1. ,  1. , -0.5],\n       [-0.5, -0.5,  1. ]])"},{"in":"array([[1., 5.],\n       [2., 5.],\n       [3., 5.]])","out":"array([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 1.]])"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/joint-rvs/covariance/","t":"Covariance","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/machine-learning/feature-engineering/feature-selection/","t":"Feature selection","c":"Machine Learning"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/machine-learning/recommender-systems/collaborative-filtering-with-neighbourhoods/","t":"Collaborative filtering with neighbourhoods","c":"Machine Learning"}],"related":["correlation-significance-test","pca-projection","cosine-similarity-matrix"],"ntests":7,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0, 3.0], [2.0, 4.0, 1.0], [3.0, 6.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.9999999999999998, -0.4999999999999999], [0.9999999999999998, 1.0, -0.4999999999999999], [-0.4999999999999999, -0.4999999999999999, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 5.0], [2.0, 5.0], [3.0, 5.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, -0.9999999999999998], [-0.9999999999999998, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[100000001.0, -3.0], [100000002.0, -5.0], [100000004.0, -6.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, -0.9285714285714288], [-0.9285714285714288, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[2.0], [7.0], [1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-0.8019314252534474, -1.324358995628145, -0.24836162209524854, -1.4162982451293669], [0.4204452380655215, 1.1360465324896427, 0.10970639932180819, 0.719431412833058], [-0.5526473205362324, -0.7847803553442784, 0.7487457707345911, -1.0795424086801158], [1.6347830429585775, 0.27276877584472176, -1.2333286640307717, 3.2389223932319675], [-0.9582652054360887, 1.6000190889991115, 0.2028824405086084, -2.022456020854962], [-1.7321348424395848, -0.08369619281702581, -1.1632259734447485, -3.5668544048228292], [-0.6292880940615545, -0.48800582327685743, -0.7133133716322436, -1.2601047024396452], [0.5533784703532895, -0.06308597192528916, -0.5894312580326048, 1.1501432818265958], [0.40963782655711695, 0.8298553070613239, -1.643023371405677, 0.7661166710399308], [-0.256730126365494, -0.9807473560440125, -0.17315522486203205, -0.5080211982950388], [-1.2894187467538587, 0.0206903940375912, -0.03788574104406823, -2.50661662806